<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bou Inania Madrasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1350–1355</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bou Inania <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">Madrasa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, was built between 1350 and 1355 by the Marinid sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-inan-faris">Abu Inan Faris</a>, after whom it is named. Unusually for a madrasa, it also served as a congregational mosque, with its own minaret. Its decoration of tile, stucco and carved cedar and its water clock make it one of the finest Marinid buildings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bou-inania-madrasa-fez.jpg" alt="Bou Inania Madrasa" width="320" height="545" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This Photo was taken by Timothy A. Gonsalves. Feel free to use my photos, but please mention me as the author. I woul... · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Courtyard_Minaret_Bou_Inania_Fes_Nov25_A7CR_09138-42_HDR3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
